1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,360
ExecutorService taught us to submit work
to a pool instead of inventing threads by
hand. That solves concurrency budgeting.

2
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:17,560
It does not yet answer a hunger that
appears the moment the task computes
something you need: how do I get a result
back, wait with a timeout, and cancel when
the caller no longer cares?

3
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:32,620
Runnable returns nothing and cannot throw
checked exceptions. Many real tasks need
both. That is why Callable exists — and
why Future is the handle you hold while
the pool does the work. Look at this java
for a moment.

4
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:45,420
submit accepts a Callable that returns an
Integer. The method returns a Future
immediately; the computation may still be
running. get(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS) blocks
for at most one second.

5
00:00:45,539 --> 00:00:55,060
If the task finishes in time, you receive
the value. If it does not, you get a
timeout instead of hanging forever. Always
prefer timeouts on get in server code.

6
00:00:55,179 --> 00:01:07,060
A bare get() with no limit is how one
stuck worker becomes a stuck request
thread — and then a stuck thread pool.
Cancellation is cooperative, not magical:
Look at this java for a moment.

7
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:14,940
cancel(true) asks the pool to interrupt
the running task if it has already
started, or to prevent it from starting if
it is still queued.

8
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:23,659
"Cooperative" means the task must notice
the interrupt — by exiting a blocking call
that throws InterruptedException, or by
checking interrupted status in a loop.

9
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:36,299
Assuming cancel instantly stops arbitrary
CPU work is a common production
disappointment. Cancellation is a request.
Well-behaved tasks honor it; stubborn
tight loops ignore it.

10
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:49,540
Callable versus Runnable is the short form
of today's design choice. Callable returns
a value and may throw checked exceptions
wrapped by the future machinery. Runnable
is for fire-and-forget side effects.

11
00:01:49,659 --> 00:01:59,699
If you find yourself stuffing results into
a shared mutable field from a Runnable,
you are reinventing Future badly.
Exceptions from the task surface when you
call get.

12
00:01:59,819 --> 00:02:07,459
They arrive wrapped — typically as
ExecutionException with the original cause
inside. Your wait site is also your
failure-handling site.

13
00:02:07,579 --> 00:02:21,180
Ignoring the cause and only logging
"execution failed" throws away the
forensic trail we fought to keep in the
exceptions episode. What if we skip
futures and only use shared variables?
Look at this java for a moment.

14
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:27,500
You still need a completion protocol.
Future is that protocol with timeouts and
cancellation already designed.

15
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:38,460
A practical pattern in services is
"submit, then wait with timeout, then
cancel on timeout." The timeout protects
the caller. The cancel attempt protects
the pool from continuing useless work.

16
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:49,660
Neither alone is a complete story;
together they are the minimum polite
concurrent call. Picture a payment
authorization callable that hits a slow
bank link. The request thread waits with a
two-second get.

17
00:02:49,780 --> 00:03:00,379
On timeout it cancels and returns "try
again." On success it continues the order.
On ExecutionException it unwraps the cause
and maps it to an API error.

18
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:13,939
That three-branch wait site is the
everyday craft — not the lambda syntax,
but the policy around waiting. Composition
pressure appears even with one future. You
might wish the transformation happened
asynchronously too.

19
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:25,460
That wish is how teams graduate to
CompletableFuture. For now, keep the
contract crisp: submit returns a handle;
the handle offers timed wait, cancel, and
exception delivery.

20
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:37,340
What if every task is fire-and-forget
logging? Then Runnable and execute may be
enough. Futures are for results and
failure delivery. Matching the tool to the
need keeps APIs honest.

21
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:43,939
Always shut down the executor with a
defined owner. Futures do not excuse
leaked executors.

22
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:56,219
The result handle and the worker pool are
partners; lifecycle belongs to the pool's
owner, while the future belongs to the
caller waiting for a value. Prefer
timeouts at every boundary where a peer
can stall.

23
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:06,939
Once many threads share maps and queues,
another question appears: can the
collections themselves participate in
thread safety, or must every access sit
inside our own locks?
